      {
        "dom_id": "question-222091",
        "name": "Question 4",
        "problem": "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-6{x}^{2}+36x-50?$\n\nThe coordinates of the vertex are\n$($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
        "worked_solution": "The parabola's vertex lies on the symmetry axis, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-6$ and $b=36$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{36}{2(-6)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{36}{(-12)} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=3$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-6{x}^{2}+36x-50 \\\\  & =-6(3)^{2}+36(3)-50 \\\\  & =-54+108-50 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(3,4).$",
        "problem_field_keys": [
          "field-1",
          "field-2"
        ],
        "answer_fields_complete": true,
        "result": "Incorrect",
