        "worked_solution": "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, we will set our result equal to $\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2k & =12 \\\\ k & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "can_dont_know": false,
        "can_skip": false,
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "step-q222099",
          "name": "Question 5",
          "problem": "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}+kx-5$ is $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Determine the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}",
          "worked_solution": "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, we will set our result equal to $\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2k & =12 \\\\ k & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
